.th DTP V 30/6/78
.sh NAME
dtp \*- mag tape format
.sh DESCRIPTION
The
command
.it dtp
dumps files to and extracts files from
magtape at 800 or 1600 bpi.
.br
The tape consists of a series of 16384 byte records (32 disk blocks)
followed by a tape mark. This is the largest record which is a power of
two that can be written by DEC tape units at 800 bpi. Records of 32768 are
possible at 1600 bpi. The first record and possibly subsequent records
contain the tape directory. Its format is as follows.
.s3
.nf
\fBstruct	dir_ent
{
	char	name[114];	/* file name */
	int	mode;		/* access modes */
	int	uid;		/* file owner */
	long	size;		/* size of file in bytes */
	long	modtime;		/* last mod time */
	int	tapea;		/* tape address */
}	dir_rec[128];\fR
.fi
.s3
.it Name
is the pathname where the file was found,
.it mode
are the permission modes of the file etc.
.it Tapea
is the tape address of the file. It is a 512 byte block number
which is independent of the tape blocking.
.br
The first entry in the first tape block is special. It contains information
about the rest of the tape. Its format is
.s3
.nf
\fBstruct	tape_header
{
	int	nfiles;		/* number of files */
	long	dumptime;		/* tape creation time */
	int	ndirblks;		/* number of directory records */
	char	pad[120];		/* unused */
}\fR
.fi
.s3
There is always at least one record in the directory. The actual number
can be determined by reading the first one.
.br
After the directory there are sufficient records to contain all the files
that have been dumped. Files start on a 512 byte boundary in the record
and extend for (size+511)/512 continuous blocks, which may extend
over several tape records.
Files are dumped in the same order as they
are in the directory. Thus the first file starts at the first data block.
This can be used to distribute tapes created by
.it dtp
as the dtp program can be the first file and can be extracted by other means
(eg. dd (I)).
.sh "SEE ALSO"
dtp (I), file system (V), ht (IV)
